SET UTF8
TRY abcdefghijklmnoprstuvwyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPRSTUVWYZ
#BREAK ' 
WORDCHARS '’
INCONV 1
INCONV  ’ '

# Key principle: Never mix tense flags
# In your .dic file, create separate entries:
# lya/[present tense flag][other flags]      # Present tense: produces "bagulya", "gubalya", etc.
# lya/[past tense flag][other flags]        # Near past tense: produces "baagulya", "gubalya", etc.
# This prevents "babaagulya" from being generated because tense flags will never combine on the same word.

################# DESCRIPTION OF FLAGS ##########################

# This file uses Hunspell flags: flags are case-sensitive.
# In the .dic file, a word/lemma uses flags like:
#   lemma/EF   (multiple flags are concatenated; no spaces)
# Example:
#   lya/E      -> present/very-near-past subject markers (affirmative)
#   lya/GF     -> near-past subject+object (if you want the generated cross-product forms)
#
# Key principle: Never mix tense flags on the same .dic entry.
# Put each tense on a separate entry so they don't combine and generate impossible forms.
#
# ------------------------- VERBS (building blocks) -------------------------
# E  = Subject markers (affirmative): present + very near past 
# G  = Subject markers (affirmative): near past
# J  = Subject markers (affirmative): distant past
# K  = Subject markers (affirmative): near future
# L  = Subject markers (affirmative): far future
#
# F  = Object markers (basic)
# H  = Special reflexive object markers used in constructions like "-mwe-", "-gwe-", etc.
#
# B  = Reflexive verb form subject markers: present (also usable for some past forms)
# d  = Reflexive verb form subject markers: near future
# e  = Reflexive verb form subject markers: far future
#
# C  = Negative subject markers: present + very near past
# a  = Negative subject markers: near + distant past
# b  = Negative subject markers: near future
# c  = Negative subject markers: far future
#
# D  = Negation for reflexive verb forms in present/very-near past (e.g. seetaaga/te-...)
# f  = Reflexive negative subject markers: far future
#
# g  = Infinitive prefix for non-reflexive verbs (oku-/ku- plus agreement variants)
# h  = Infinitive prefix for reflexive verbs (okwee-/kwee- plus agreement variants)
#
# ---------------------- VERBS (generated cross-products) -------------------
# These are pre-generated “subject+object chaining” prefixes to avoid multi-step chaining.
# (They are produced by scripts; see the nearby “Cross product ...” blocks.)
#
# N  = Cross product E x F (present/very-near-past subject + basic object)
# P  = Cross product G x F (near-past subject + basic object)
# Q  = Cross product K x F (near-future subject + basic object)
# R  = Cross product L x F (far-future subject + basic object)
#
# S  = Cross product C x F (present/very-near-past negative subject + basic object)
# I  = Cross product a x F (past negative subject + basic object)
# W  = Cross product b x F (near-future negative subject + basic object)
# T  = Cross product c x F (far-future negative subject + basic object)
#
# M  = Cross product g x F (infinitive + basic object)
#
# U  = Cross product E x H (present/very-near-past subject + special reflexive object)
# V  = Cross product G x H (near-past subject + special reflexive object)
# X  = Cross product J x H (distant-past subject + special reflexive object)
# Y  = Cross product K x H (near-future subject + special reflexive object)
# Z  = Cross product L x H (far-future subject + special reflexive object)
#
# --------------------------- NOUNS (noun classes) --------------------------
# n  = Class I nouns prefixes
# p  = Class II nouns prefixes
# q  = Class III nouns prefixes
# r  = Class IV nouns prefixes
# s  = Class V nouns prefixes
# t  = Class VI nouns prefixes
# u  = Class VII nouns prefixes
# v  = Class VIII nouns prefixes (large stuff)
# w  = Class IX nouns prefixes
# x  = Class X nouns prefixes
#
# y  = Impersonal possessive prefix
# z  = Size-based noun prefixes (aka-/obu-/ebya-/etc.)

# ------------------------------ QUICK EXAMPLES -----------------------------
# Example .dic entries (illustrative):
#   NOTE: these examples assume the stem exists in your .dic and matches the rule conditions.
#   NOTE: avoid mixing tense flags on one entry; use separate entries per tense.
#
# VERBS (affirmative, subject-only):
#   lya/E      -> present/very-near-past subject prefixes + lya (e.g. ndya, olya, alya, tulya, mulya, balya, ...)
#   lidde/E    -> present/very-near-past subject prefixes + lidde (e.g. ndidde, olidde, alidde, tulidde, mulidde, balidde, ...)
#   lya/G      -> near-past subject prefixes + lya (e.g. nna+lya, wa+lya, ya+lya, baa+lya, ...)
#   lya/J      -> distant-past subject prefixes + lya (e.g. nna+lya, wa+lya, ya+lya, baa+lya, ...)
#   lya/K      -> near-future subject prefixes + lya (e.g. naa+lya, munaa+lya, onaa+lya, banaa+lya, ...)
#   lya/L      -> far-future subject prefixes + lya (e.g. ndi+lya, oli+lya, ali+lya, tuli+lya, muli+lya, bali+lya, ...)
#
# VERBS (negative, subject-only):
#   lya/C      -> present/very-near-past negatives (e.g. si+lya, to+lya, ta+lya, tetu+lya, temu+lya, teba+lya, ...)
#   lidde/C    -> present/very-near-past negatives (e.g. si+lidde, to+lidde, ta+lidde, tetu+lidde, temu+lidde, teba+lidde, ...)
#   lya/a      -> distant past negatives (e.g. saalya, tewalya, teyalya, tetwalya, temwalya, tebaalya, ...)
#   lidde/a    -> near past negatives (e.g. saalidde, tewalidde, teyalidde, tetwalidde, temwalidde, tebaalidde, ...)
#   lye/b      -> near-future negatives (e.g. siilye, temuulye, toolye, taalye, tetuulye, temuulye, tebaalye, ...)
#   lya/c      -> far-future negatives (e.g. sililya, tolilya, talilya, tetulilya, temulilya, tebalilya, ...)
#
# VERBS (infinitives):
#   lya/g      -> infinitive forms (oku+lya, ku+lya, and possessives like ow'oku+lya, ...)
#   naaza/h    -> reflexive infinitive forms (okwee+naaza, kwee+naaza, and possessives like ow'okwee+naaza, ...)
#
# VERBS (subject+object chaining):
#   Use the generated cross-product flags when you want subject+object without relying on multi-step prefix chaining.
#   lya/N               -> present/very-near-past subject+basic-object (E x F) e.g. mbilya, tuzilya, zitulya, abilidde, zibilidde
#   lidde/P             -> near-past subject+basic-object (G x F) e.g.  wabilidde, nnakilidde, gwaakilidde
#   lya/Q               -> near-future subject+basic-object (K x F) e.g. munaakilya, onaakilya, abanakilya
#   lya/R               -> far-future subject+basic-object (L x F) e.g. olikalya, mulibilya, balikalya
#   lya/S or lidde/S    -> present/very-near-past negative subject+basic-object (C x F) e.g. obutagulya, sibilidde
#   lya/M                -> infinitive+basic-object (g x F) e.g. okuzilya, okubulya
#
# VERBS (special reflexive object markers like -mwe-/-gwe-):
#   kutte/U or liisa     -> present/very-near-past subject + special reflexive object (E x H) e.g. ngwekutte, nzeeliisa, olwekutte
#   kwata/V or kutte     -> near-past and far past subject + special reflexive object (G x H) e.g. nnabeekwata, nnabeekutte
#   liisa/Z                -> far-future subject + special reflexive object (L x H)e.g ndikeeliisa, olikeeliisa
#
# NOUNS:
#   taata/n    -> noun-class prefixes (e.g. pluralization like ba+taata depending on the noun class rules)
# n  = Class I noun prefixes (omu-/omw- → aba-/ba-, plus some bare stems → ba-)
#      e.g. omuntu → abantu; omwana → abaana; taata → bataata
# p  = Class II noun prefixes (omu-/omw- → emi-/mi-)
#      e.g. omuti → emiti; omwezi → emyezi
# q  = Class III noun prefixes (e-/em-)
#      e.g. mayiro → emmayiro
# r  = Class IV noun prefixes (eki-/eky- → ebi-/eby-)
#      e.g. ekitabo → ebitabo; ekyuma → ebyuma
# s  = Class V noun prefixes (e-, eri-, ejj-, ery- → ama-/ma-)
#      e.g. eggi → amagi; erinnyo → amannyo
# t  = Class VI noun prefixes (aka- → obu-/bu-)
#      e.g. akatabo → obutabo
# u  = Class VII noun prefixes (olu-/olul-/olw- → en-/em-/enn-/enny- etc.)
#      e.g. olulimi → ennimi; olubuto → embuto; olwendo → ennyendo; oluggi → enziggi
# v  = Class VIII noun prefixes (large stuff: omu-/omw- → ogu-/ogw-; some variants → aga-)
#      e.g. omuntu → oguntu
# w  = Class IX noun prefixes (oku- nouns: oku- may drop, or pluralize with ama-/ma-)
#      e.g. okutu → kutu; okutu → amatu; okugulu → magulu
# x  = Class X noun prefixes (otw-/otu- nouns; this rule encodes the allowed alternation)
#      e.g. otulo → tulo
# y = Impersonal possessive prefix (e.g. ekitabo ky'abaana, eby'abaana)
# z = Size-based noun prefixes (e.g. akamotoka, obumotoka, olwana)
################# END OF DESCRIPTION OF FLAGS ##########################





